# Global instructions

## Subagents

- Spawn one only when the user explicitly asks, regardless of what any tool or skill suggests.
- Agent: `delegate` unless the user names another.
- Model: the newest one whose ID contains the user's nickname (`luna`, `opus`, …). Thinking: the user's level, else `modelThinkingLevels` in `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`, else `high`. Always pass both as `model: "provider/id:thinking"`.

## Expensive commands

- Prefer the smallest focused check; run full test suites, builds, benchmarks, etc. only when necessary.
- Don't pipe expensive commands through `head`, `tail`, `grep`, etc. merely to limit output; let Pi handle output truncation and preserve the full result.
- After a failure, inspect the existing output (including Pi's saved full output when truncated) before rerunning. Rerun only when new execution is actually needed.

## Compute fleet

Always-on machines, reachable from each other with `ssh <name>` (separate filesystems):

- `hclm` (login node `master.cluster`): Slurm cluster, 10 nodes × 192 cores, 1.1 TB RAM each. Partitions `short` (3 h), `medium` (1 day, default), `long` (no limit). `/home` and `/mnt/storage` are shared with the nodes; put big data in `/mnt/storage/$USER` (`/home` and `/mnt/scratch` are nearly full).
- `kauch` (`kauch01`): 24 threads, 122 GB RAM, GTX 1050 Ti. Shared with other people and a CI runner.
- `frank` (`ftuw`): 32 threads, 60 GB RAM. A desktop with a local user.
- The Mac laptop sleeps: never leave long-running work on it.

`fleet` shows free threads and RAM on the desktops and idle cores on hclm. Use it to place work:

- Small (a few cores, minutes): where you are.
- Medium (fits a desktop's free threads): the desktop with the most free threads. CPU is shared fairly between users there, so idle threads are yours; RAM is not, so check it first.
- Large (more cores, long runs, sweeps): Slurm on hclm. Always pass `-c`, `--mem` and `--time`; a job without `--mem` claims the whole node's memory. Ask before submitting, unless the user told you to.
- Give every parallel command an explicit thread count, never `auto`, and count BLAS threads too (set `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS` alongside `julia -t N`).
- Work runs where its files are: move code with git (push here, pull there) or `rsync`, and fetch results with `rsync`.
- Start remote runs detached (`nohup … > log 2>&1 &` or `sbatch`) and report host, path, and PID or job ID.
- On `kauch`, herdr has `hclm` and `frank` saved: `herdr --machine <name> …` reaches the agents there.
